A||||5||Carolina 88GT||Rear Windshield Glass (TOTALY FRUSTRATED)||||8||
Z||000000||Carolina 88GT||06-14-2000||02:57 PM||||I need some help/advice.<P>Had the rear glass broken out of my car.  Have taken it to 3 damn glass places who said "yeah, we can replace that" only to be told that they will have to remove the trunk lid to do it.  Finally I said...okay f*** it...go ahead and remove the trunk lid.  Well they did that and now they are telling me bout some kind of hinge loaded spring(s) that will have to come off now as well.  Somebody tell me what the hell is going on!||||reg||8
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||06-14-2000||03:24 PM||||Taking off the trunklid is not a big deal. 4 bolts and its off. Realigning the lid is not difficult. The hinges they are talking about are the torque bars that keep the lid up when you open it. Lot of tension in those bars. Since the rear window does extend down pretty far below the rear decklid it is not an unusual request. If they  take it off they should be able to put it back on. Its not easy but its doable.<P>Hope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||turbo86se||06-14-2000||07:38 PM||||Carolina, if you need a rear window, LMK, I have one out and in a box.  It's got the rear defroster and is in excellent shape.  If you want it, I'll sell it cheap!!  It's just sitting in storage down in Georgetown!!  <BR>-joel   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||06-14-2000||08:25 PM||||You DONT need to remove the torsion bars to take the lid of. Its only 4 bolts. The springs will stay with the hinges against the stops. Since it is not the lid that is the stop they done have to be messed with. I didnt every remove the lid when I replaced mine and it was not broken I had to take a good glass out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carolina 88GT||06-14-2000||08:25 PM||||Turbo!  Thanks for the offer dude....I already have the glass....just need them to figure out how they are going to install it.  Get this....I called the Pontiac dealer and they were like....Ummmmm....uhh....Gee, I dont know.  So the glass man has borrowed a repair manual on it.....Somehow I see a bad karma?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Guinea Pig||06-15-2000||01:37 AM||||Had to replace the rear window after the St. Louis (Collinsville) National.  The Instructions are in the Haynes and Chilton manuals.  If you remove the deck lid, mark your position and replace it on the same marks.  The torsion springs had to be blocked with short 2X2 wooden scraps to keep them from snaping into the window.  If it's already broken, it shouldn't be that critical, except it could scatter glass into the interior.  The deck may not have to be removed, but it made it a lot easier to remove and replace the glass.  Much of the interior-- firewall cover, seats, side panals(where the seat belts rollers are)and the headliner were removed.  My window had been shattered by vandals, and removal of the seats made for easier movement inside the car to remove the glass still attached to the sealant surrounding the window opening. For my limited ability I needed all the ease I could get. ||||reg||1
